      "source": [
        "# 🏗️ Topic 2: Prompt Layers as Messages in Code\n",
        "\n",
        "Now you stop reading layers and start **writing** them, on the customer's question.\n",
        "\n",
        "When you call a model from your own code (as in Notebook 1), the layers are simply **messages in a list**, in order. The model reads the list from top to bottom:\n",
        "\n",
        "```python\n",
        "messages = [\n",
        "    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": HARNESS_RULES + BANK_RULES},   # layers 3 + 2\n",
        "    {\"role\": \"user\",   \"content\": CUSTOMER_MESSAGE},                # layer 1\n",
        "]\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A **system message** holds standing instructions: they sit in front of every conversation, and nobody retypes them. Layer 2 (the app's rules) and layer 3 (the harness's rules) both go there, with the harness's rules first.\n",
        "\n",
        "The key idea of this topic: **the same model, asked the same question, gives different answers depending on the layers in front of the question.** A model can look smart or useless depending on the text around it.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🔍 What You'll Learn\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Layer 1 alone:** how the model answers the customer with no instructions at all.\n",
        "- **Layer 2:** how to write a short user system prompt yourself, in two or three plain sentences.\n",
        "- **Layer 3:** what happens when a harness's rules sit in front of yours. Here we use the **coding agent's** rules from Exercise 1, so you can see what a harness written for a different job does to a bank answer.\n",
        "- That every layer you add is **paid for on every call**: watch the tokens in.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🧠 Why This Matters\n",
        "\n",
        "- Most of your daily prompt work will be in layer 2. Knowing that it sits under layer 3 explains surprises: your rules are not the only rules the model reads.\n",
        "- Often \"a better AI\" is really a better harness around the same model."
      ]
    },
    {
