      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "4188a258",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4188a258"
      },
      "source": [
        "# 🧩 Topic 4: Who Wrote Each Layer, and What the Model Really Did\n",
        "\n",
        "Two questions finish the picture of this notebook.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Who wrote it?** Every sentence the model reads has an author. Ask *\"who would write this, and how often does it change?\"* and you find the layer. A sentence about this one product (like a rule about card numbers) belongs to the app's builders: layer 2. A sentence about how agents work in general (like how to handle a failed tool) belongs to the harness: layer 3.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Who did it?** In everything you ran in this notebook, the model did only one kind of thing: it **wrote text** (answers and a plan). Everything else (putting the layers in order, putting the plan back, counting tokens, adding up the bill) was **ordinary software**.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🔍 What You'll Learn\n",
        "\n",
        "- To find the **layer** of any sentence from its author.\n",
        "- To say, for each step of your own run, whether the **model** or the **code** did it.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 🧠 Why This Matters\n",
        "\n",
        "- \"Know your layer before you change a word.\" It saves hours.\n",
        "- Seeing the model as one part among many is the mindset of the whole course: you build the software around the model."
      ]
    },
